From 999337e38d416fad49993a4dd33fe44225e6a85c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yichao Yu <yyc1992@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 01:01:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 01/21] util: BLAS/LAPACK detection at configure time, with
 built-in fallback

Add src/util/nlopt-blas.h, through which algorithms call the dense
linear algebra routines they need as NLOPT_BLAS(name)(...) with the
Fortran calling convention, either from an external BLAS/LAPACK or from
built-in versions.

New CMake option NLOPT_BLAS (case-insensitive): AUTO (default) uses a
BLAS/LAPACK found by FindLAPACK if it provides all the routines in
nlopt-blas.h, ON requires one, OFF never uses one, and any other value
is passed as the library list (names, paths or flags) to link against.
The Fortran symbol suffix ("_64_", "_64", "_" or none) is found by
test-linking all the routines, without running anything, so this works
when cross-compiling; a "64" suffix means 64-bit integers.  Libraries
that provide both interfaces get the one matching the pointer size.
The libraries are added to Libs.private in nlopt.pc.

Without a BLAS/LAPACK, nlopt-blas-builtin.h provides plain C ports of
the reference BLAS/LAPACK 3.12 routines (pointer-sized integers),
restricted to the arguments used (e.g. DGELSY without column pivoting
and with the basic instead of the minimum-norm solution when rank
deficient).  They are static inline, so that each algorithm can inline
them.  Compared with OpenBLAS on random inputs they agree to rounding
and make the same rank decisions.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
